---
# clang-tidy config for giga_edge.
# Philosophy: OPT-IN. Every enabled check is listed explicitly — there are no family wildcards
# for the lint groups (bugprone / performance / modernize / readability / misc / google). A
# clang-tidy upgrade therefore never silently turns on a new check: adding one is a deliberate
# edit here, and the active set is fully visible.
#
# clang-analyzer is the one exception: it is enabled by *relevant subfamily* rather than per
# checker, because its checkers are static-analyzer engine checks (not style lints) and self-gate
# by language. Enabled subfamilies: core, cplusplus, deadcode, nullability, security, unix, and
# the portable optin.* checkers (optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange, optin.cplusplus.*,
# optin.performance.*, optin.portability.UnixAPI).
#   Intentionally omitted analyzer subfamilies (they never fire on our C++ and only add noise to
#   the enabled-check list): osx.* / osx.cocoa.* / osx.coreFoundation.* / optin.osx.* (Apple/ObjC),
#   fuchsia.* (Fuchsia OS), optin.mpi.* (MPI), webkit.* (WebKit ref-counting idioms),
#   optin.taint.* (experimental taint analysis, high false-positive rate). apiModeling.* is also
#   left off: those are non-diagnostic modeling checkers the analyzer pulls in as dependencies on
#   its own, so omitting them changes nothing.
#
# Checks deliberately NOT enabled, with the reasons. The reasons live in this header, NOT inside
# the folded `Checks:` scalar below: YAML treats `#` inside a folded scalar as literal text, which
# silently corrupts the check list.
#
# Why no bugprone-exception-escape: it flags "an exception may escape a noexcept function /
# destructor", but here that is almost always a false positive — destructors log errors via
# std::cerr (they do not actually throw) and the analyzer cannot prove noexcept transitively.
# Noisy without practical benefit.
#
# Why no performance-unnecessary-value-param: a low-signal perf nag — it flags by-value params
# that could be const-ref. It false-positives on moved-from sinks whose move sits on a path
# the analyzer can't prove unreachable (e.g. after a throw), and otherwise only churns
# signatures for marginal copy elimination. Not worth the noise.
#
# Why no performance-inefficient-string-concatenation: flags chained operator+ that builds
# temporaries. In practice it fires on one-shot error-message construction in throw paths,
# where readability outweighs a copy that runs once on failure.
#
# Why no performance-avoid-endl: rewrites std::endl to '\n', dropping the flush. Here std::endl
# is used on diagnostic/error output where the flush is intentional (get the message out
# before a possible crash/abort); on these cold paths the rewrite changes behavior for no
# real gain.
#
# Why no bugprone-unchecked-optional-access: flags every optional access (operator-> / .value())
# the local flow can't prove guarded. But safety here is normally established by a class invariant
# or a precondition set elsewhere (an accessor whose contract is "only call once populated", or a
# helper reached only from a has_value() else-branch) — invariants this check cannot see across
# functions. It thus false-positives on provably-safe access and forces NOLINT clutter or redundant
# re-checks; and .value() already throws on empty, so it is not even UB. Noisy.
#
# Why no bugprone-inc-dec-in-conditions: flags reading and incrementing/decrementing the same
# variable inside one compound condition. In our cases this is the classic in-place rstrip from
# the vendored inih parser — `while (p > s && isspace((unsigned char)(*--p)))` — which is correct:
# the `&&` sequence point fully evaluates `p > s` before `--p`, so there is no UB. A stylistic
# false positive on idiomatic C; not worth the NOLINT clutter on vendored code.
#
# Why no bugprone-branch-clone: it flags "repeated branch body in conditional chain", but
# false-positives on dispatch chains where each branch assigns a distinct constant — e.g. a
# mapping of dtypes to the constants of a backend (int8 -> its INT8, int16 -> its INT16, ...): the bodies
# are structurally identical yet semantically distinct, and flattening them (table/macro) hurts
# readability for no correctness gain. Noisy on these idiomatic mappings.
#
# Also left out of bugprone (low-signal / high-noise for this codebase): easily-swappable-parameters,
# narrowing-conversions, implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result, chained-comparison,
# throwing-static-initialization. Out of performance: no-int-to-ptr, type-promotion-in-math-fn,
# enum-size. misc-include-cleaner is not enabled (it demands an include-what-you-use discipline we
# don't follow).
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-argument-comment,
  bugprone-assert-side-effect,
  bugprone-assignment-in-if-condition,
  bugprone-bad-signal-to-kill-thread,
  bugprone-bitwise-pointer-cast,
  bugprone-bool-pointer-implicit-conversion,
  bugprone-capturing-this-in-member-variable,
  bugprone-casting-through-void,
  bugprone-command-processor,
  bugprone-compare-pointer-to-member-virtual-function,
  bugprone-copy-constructor-init,
  bugprone-copy-constructor-mutates-argument,
  bugprone-crtp-constructor-accessibility,
  bugprone-dangling-handle,
  bugprone-default-operator-new-on-overaligned-type,
  bugprone-derived-method-shadowing-base-method,
  bugprone-dynamic-static-initializers,
  bugprone-empty-catch,
  bugprone-exception-copy-constructor-throws,
  bugprone-float-loop-counter,
  bugprone-fold-init-type,
  bugprone-forward-declaration-namespace,
  bugprone-forwarding-reference-overload,
  bugprone-inaccurate-erase,
  bugprone-incorrect-enable-if,
  bugprone-incorrect-enable-shared-from-this,
  bugprone-incorrect-roundings,
  bugprone-infinite-loop,
  bugprone-integer-division,
  bugprone-invalid-enum-default-initialization,
  bugprone-lambda-function-name,
  bugprone-macro-parentheses,
  bugprone-macro-repeated-side-effects,
  bugprone-misleading-setter-of-reference,
  bugprone-misplaced-operator-in-strlen-in-alloc,
  bugprone-misplaced-pointer-arithmetic-in-alloc,
  bugprone-misplaced-widening-cast,
  bugprone-move-forwarding-reference,
  bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion,
  bugprone-multiple-new-in-one-expression,
  bugprone-multiple-statement-macro,
  bugprone-no-escape,
  bugprone-non-zero-enum-to-bool-conversion,
  bugprone-nondeterministic-pointer-iteration-order,
  bugprone-not-null-terminated-result,
  bugprone-optional-value-conversion,
  bugprone-parent-virtual-call,
  bugprone-pointer-arithmetic-on-polymorphic-object,
  bugprone-posix-return,
  bugprone-random-generator-seed,
  bugprone-raw-memory-call-on-non-trivial-type,
  bugprone-redundant-branch-condition,
  bugprone-reserved-identifier,
  bugprone-return-const-ref-from-parameter,
  bugprone-shared-ptr-array-mismatch,
  bugprone-signal-handler,
  bugprone-signed-char-misuse,
  bugprone-sizeof-container,
  bugprone-sizeof-expression,
  bugprone-spuriously-wake-up-functions,
  bugprone-standalone-empty,
  bugprone-std-namespace-modification,
  bugprone-string-constructor,
  bugprone-string-integer-assignment,
  bugprone-string-literal-with-embedded-nul,
  bugprone-stringview-nullptr,
  bugprone-suspicious-enum-usage,
  bugprone-suspicious-include,
  bugprone-suspicious-memory-comparison,
  bugprone-suspicious-memset-usage,
  bugprone-suspicious-missing-comma,
  bugprone-suspicious-realloc-usage,
  bugprone-suspicious-semicolon,
  bugprone-suspicious-string-compare,
  bugprone-suspicious-stringview-data-usage,
  bugprone-swapped-arguments,
  bugprone-switch-missing-default-case,
  bugprone-tagged-union-member-count,
  bugprone-terminating-continue,
  bugprone-throw-keyword-missing,
  bugprone-too-small-loop-variable,
  bugprone-unchecked-string-to-number-conversion,
  bugprone-undefined-memory-manipulation,
  bugprone-undelegated-constructor,
  bugprone-unhandled-exception-at-new,
  bugprone-unhandled-self-assignment,
  bugprone-unintended-char-ostream-output,
  bugprone-unique-ptr-array-mismatch,
  bugprone-unsafe-functions,
  bugprone-unused-local-non-trivial-variable,
  bugprone-unused-raii,
  bugprone-unused-return-value,
  bugprone-use-after-move,
  bugprone-virtual-near-miss,
  performance-faster-string-find,
  performance-for-range-copy,
  performance-implicit-conversion-in-loop,
  performance-inefficient-algorithm,
  performance-inefficient-vector-operation,
  performance-move-const-arg,
  performance-move-constructor-init,
  performance-no-automatic-move,
  performance-noexcept-destructor,
  performance-noexcept-move-constructor,
  performance-noexcept-swap,
  performance-trivially-destructible,
  performance-unnecessary-copy-initialization,
  clang-analyzer-core.*,
  clang-analyzer-cplusplus.*,
  clang-analyzer-deadcode.DeadStores,
  clang-analyzer-nullability.*,
  clang-analyzer-security.*,
  clang-analyzer-unix.*,
  clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange,
  clang-analyzer-optin.cplusplus.*,
  clang-analyzer-optin.performance.*,
  clang-analyzer-optin.portability.UnixAPI,
  modernize-use-override,
  modernize-use-nullptr,
  modernize-use-equals-default,
  modernize-use-equals-delete,
  modernize-use-emplace,
  modernize-make-unique,
  modernize-make-shared,
  modernize-use-bool-literals,
  modernize-loop-convert,
  modernize-redundant-void-arg,
  modernize-use-using,
  modernize-deprecated-headers,
  modernize-shrink-to-fit,
  readability-container-size-empty,
  readability-redundant-string-cstr,
  readability-redundant-smartptr-get,
  readability-redundant-control-flow,
  readability-redundant-member-init,
  readability-misleading-indentation,
  readability-static-accessed-through-instance,
  readability-string-compare,
  readability-duplicate-include,
  readability-identifier-naming,
  misc-unused-using-decls,
  misc-unused-alias-decls,
  misc-redundant-expression,
  google-explicit-constructor,
  google-build-using-namespace

WarningsAsErrors: ''

HeaderFilterRegex: '.*'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '(/contrib/|/downloads/|/install/|/_deps/|/\.cpm/|/build_[^/]*/)'

CheckOptions:
  readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.StructCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.UnionCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.TypedefCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.MethodCase: 'CamelCase'
  readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberSuffix: '_'
  readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberSuffix: '_'
  readability-identifier-naming.PublicMemberCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.ParameterCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.LocalVariableCase: 'lower_case'
  readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantCase: 'UPPER_CASE'
  # VALUE_COUNT_ is the project-wide sentinel enumerator the enum lib recognizes; the
  # trailing underscore is the convention, not a case violation.
  readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantIgnoredRegexp: '^VALUE_COUNT_$'
  # YAML is the yaml-cpp namespace; we only forward-declare it, the spelling is not ours.
  readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceIgnoredRegexp: '^YAML$'
  # STL container-interface aliases must keep their standard lowercase spelling for
  # generic code (iterators, traits) to work.
  readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasIgnoredRegexp: '^(value_type|element_type|pointer|const_pointer|reference|const_reference|size_type|difference_type|iterator|const_iterator|iterator_category)$'
  readability-identifier-naming.TypedefIgnoredRegexp: '^(value_type|element_type|pointer|const_pointer|reference|const_reference|size_type|difference_type|iterator|const_iterator|iterator_category)$'
  # VariableCase and MacroDefinitionCase are intentionally unset:
  #  - VariableCase leaks onto UPPER_CASE constants (e.g. `static const MAX_*`), which the
  #    codebase mixes by design; const/constexpr naming is deliberately not enforced.
  #  - MacroDefinitionCase flags legitimate trailing-underscore macros (GIGA_EDGE_..._).
  # Locals and parameters are still enforced via LocalVariableCase / ParameterCase.
...
